<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <div class="pt-faq" style="max-width:800px;margin:0 auto">
    <details><summary>What is the Ayurvedic approach to sciatica treatment?</summary><p class="faq-a">Ayurveda treats sciatica by addressing the disturbance behind the nerve irritation rather than the pain alone. At KSAC Hospitals the approach begins with a precise diagnosis, including a review of the patient's MRI, since sciatica is a symptom and the cause decides the plan. The treating doctor then prepares a customised plan that may combine physician-supervised therapy, internal Ayurvedic medicines and structured rehabilitation.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Will I be given the same treatment as other sciatica patients?</summary><p class="faq-a">No. A <a href="/pages/ayurvedic-slip-disc-treatment-in-hyderabad" style="color:#3c8dbc">herniated disc</a>, a narrowed spinal canal, a slipped vertebra and a tight muscle in the buttock can all cause the same leg pain and need different plans. Two patients with similar symptoms may therefore be advised quite different treatment. The doctor decides after diagnosis and explains the plan before treatment begins.</p></details>
    <details><summary>When is sciatica an emergency?</summary><p class="faq-a">Worsening leg weakness, difficulty lifting the front of the foot, numbness around the inner thighs or genital area, any change in bladder or bowel control or sudden severe symptoms in both legs all need urgent hospital assessment the same day rather than an elective appointment. These can indicate compression of the nerve bundle at the base of the spine, where delay affects the outcome.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Do I need to bring my MRI to the consultation?</summary><p class="faq-a">Yes, where available. The MRI, any CT or X-ray reports and current prescriptions help the doctor establish exactly what is compressing the nerve and plan accordingly. Sciatica ayurveda care at KSAC Hospitals is planned around clinical findings and the patient's own reports and fresh imaging is advised only where it is needed.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Can Ayurveda address chronic sciatica that has lasted months?</summary><p class="faq-a">Long-standing sciatica usually involves a persisting structural factor along with the wider pattern behind the nerve irritation so it is assessed in detail before treatment is advised. Care at KSAC Hospitals addresses those factors through a sustained, doctor-supervised plan combining therapy, medicines and progressive rehabilitation. Response in long-standing cases is gradual and the doctor reviews progress across follow-up visits.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is Ayurvedic sciatica treatment safe alongside conventional care?</summary><p class="faq-a">Patients already receiving physiotherapy, pain medication or other prescribed treatment should tell the treating doctor at the first consultation. The doctor at KSAC Hospitals, an Ayurvedic hospital in Hyderabad, takes all current treatment into account when planning care so that the two work together. Any change to conventional medication should be discussed with the doctor who prescribed it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What lifestyle changes support sciatica recovery?</summary><p class="faq-a">Keeping good posture when sitting and standing, breaking up long periods of sitting, practising the prescribed spinal and hip exercises, maintaining a healthy weight and avoiding sudden heavy lifting or twisting all support recovery. The treating doctor provides individualised guidance based on the patient's condition and occupational demands.</p></details>
  </div>
</section>

